Subject: a bitmap holds as many bits as it holds

ext4_fs_init_block_bitmap() marks a group's head blocks in use when it
materialises a bitmap for a group flagged BLOCK_UNINIT. The count it
marks, bit_max, is built out of on-disk values -- the group count that
ext4_bg_num_gdb() derives its answer from, and s_reserved_gdt_blocks --
and the loop WRITES one bit per iteration into a block-sized buffer.

A bitmap holds 8 * block_size bits and describes blocks_per_group of
them. Beyond either is a write past the buffer, and this runs at mount,
from ext4_fs_get_block_group_ref, during an ordinary inode lookup.

    ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
      ext4_bmap_bit_set ext4_bitmap.h:54
      ext4_fs_init_block_bitmap <- ext4_fs_get_block_group_ref
      <- __ext4_fs_get_inode_ref

An out-of-bounds WRITE from mounting a volume is the worst shape this
class comes in, so the bound is a clamp rather than a refusal: the
group's real head blocks are still marked, the mount proceeds, and a
group whose descriptor claims more than its own bitmap can describe is
corrupt in a way e2fsck reports anyway. Refusing here would turn a
recoverable volume into an unmountable one for no gain.

Found by fuzzing, two separate inputs in a two-minute campaign.

Fixture Tests/fixtures/hostile/0008-a-bitmap-marked-past-its-own-end.img.gz.

--- a/src/ext4_fs.c
+++ b/src/ext4_fs.c
@@ -340,7 +340,35 @@
 		}
 	} else { /* For META_BG_BLOCK_GROUPS */
 		bit_max += ext4_bg_num_gdb(sb, bg_ref->index);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * bit_max is built out of on-disk values -- the group count that
+	 * ext4_bg_num_gdb derives its answer from, and s_reserved_gdt_blocks --
+	 * and the loop below WRITES one bit per iteration into a
+	 * block-sized buffer. A bitmap holds 8 * block_size bits and covers
+	 * blocks_per_group of them; anything beyond either is a write past the
+	 * buffer, and this runs at mount, while initialising a group flagged
+	 * BLOCK_UNINIT.
+	 *
+	 * Found by fuzzing: AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow in
+	 * ext4_bmap_bit_set, reached from ext4_fs_get_block_group_ref during
+	 * an ordinary inode lookup. An out-of-bounds write from mounting a
+	 * volume is the worst shape this class comes in, so the clamp is
+	 * unconditional rather than a refusal: the group's real head blocks
+	 * are still marked, and a group whose descriptor claims more than its
+	 * own bitmap can describe is corrupt in a way e2fsck will report.
+	 */
+	{
+		uint32_t bitmap_bits = block_size * 8;
+		uint32_t group_blocks = ext4_get32(sb, blocks_per_group);
+
+		if (group_blocks && bit_max > group_blocks)
+			bit_max = group_blocks;
+		if (bit_max > bitmap_bits)
+			bit_max = bitmap_bits;
 	}
+
 	for (bit = 0; bit < bit_max; bit++)
 		ext4_bmap_bit_set(block_bitmap.data, bit);
 
